From ca37d8485d9c5a6fea26c99a245d9af39d7f4316 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ice <icex@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 23:49:57 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] xsts: mint the token with the running title's claim, not a
 stand-in

An XSTS token can carry a title claim, and the services that scope what
they do by title -- presence's titles/current, the session directory --
refuse a token that does not carry the right one. Measured against a live
account, `POST sessiondirectory.xboxlive.com/handles/query` for a title's
service config answers:

  * 200, with a token carrying no title claim at all,
  * 403 "The requested service config cannot be accessed.", with a token
    claiming a *different* title,
  * 200, with a token claiming the title that owns the config.

So a stand-in claim is worse than none. The service was handing every
title the Xbox app's identity, which is why Goat Simulator 3 reported
"failed to create party" and set no presence while its profile, social,
achievements and stats calls all returned 200 with the same token.

The runtime now sends the running title's TitleId and MSAAppId (out of
its MicrosoftGame.config) with each token request, and the SISU identity
is minted and cached per title. Where the title is unknown the claimless
chain is used rather than a substitute, because that is the answer that
works. A relying party that refuses a title token is remembered so the
retry costs one round trip once, rather than a hardcoded list of which
audiences take one.

Two things make the failure visible next time. `api::xbox::claims` names
the routes that are scoped by title, and the service logs, at the moment
it hands back a claimless token for one of them, which of the three
possible reasons it was: no identity from the runtime, a relying party
that refused, or SISU failing. And the service's own log now defaults to
info rather than to the EnvFilter's error-only default, since it is the
only place that reason ever appears.

`xodus-cli xbl-check <game dir>` runs the same distinction on demand:
unclaimed reads first, then the claim, then a claimed read, so a failure
is attributed instead of guessed at.
